WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
Scheduling is one of the most practical ways to reduce GPU waste.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.870
When teams share a limited pool through queues or job schedulers,

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:11.461
they can prioritize important work, avoid over-allocation, and make idle capacity visible.

00:00:11.461 --> 00:00:15.000
A queue also creates a natural place to apply limits and ownership rules.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Idle shutdown policies are especially valuable for notebooks, development workers,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
test clusters, and temporary endpoints.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.500
If a resource has no useful work, no active owner, or no current schedule,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.300
it should not quietly consume budget.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.010
Shutdown should be predictable and communicated so teams can save work properly.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.100
Interruptible capacity can be useful when a job can tolerate stops and restarts.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.630
That requires checkpointing, retry limits, idempotent job behavior,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:38.995
and clear expectations.

00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:42.625
It is not a universal answer, but it can be a strong fit for some batch, training,

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:43.895
and evaluation work.

00:00:43.895 --> 00:00:47.400
Timeouts, priorities, and retry limits prevent runaway execution.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.285
A failed job should not restart forever.

00:00:49.285 --> 00:00:52.349
A low-priority experiment should not block urgent production work.

00:00:52.349 --> 00:00:55.800
A scheduled window can align expensive processing with real business need.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.685
Cleanup automation closes the loop.

00:00:57.685 --> 00:01:01.830
Volumes, temporary datasets, checkpoints, unused endpoints, stale images,

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.800
and generated artifacts should have owners and retention rules.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.495
Reducing idle time often saves money without changing the model or reducing useful output.

00:01:09.495 --> 00:01:11.125
Scheduling also protects people.

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:13.310
When queues, priorities, and windows are clear,

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:17.140
teams spend less time negotiating access informally and more time improving the work

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:17.600
itself.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.200
Predictable scheduling makes cost control feel like a normal operating practice.
